WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.759
Part three:

00:00:01.809 --> 00:00:05.582
the challenges, and the pivotal problem
behind them.

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:12.561
Every mission, scheme and department can
build its own dashboards and information

00:00:12.611 --> 00:00:13.102
systems.

00:00:13.152 --> 00:00:17.837
With so many actors, that means a large
and growing number of systems,

00:00:17.887 --> 00:00:21.963
each collecting data in its own format, at
its own frequency,

00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:24.669
with limited ways to talk to each other.

00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:25.413
The result:

00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:29.066
duplication, inconsistent data, and a
fragmented view.

00:00:30.616 --> 00:00:33.752
The coordination problems touch every
actor.

00:00:33.802 --> 00:00:39.473
Payments to employees, frontline workers
and vendors are delayed and uncertain.

00:00:39.523 --> 00:00:45.267
Unutilised funds sit in many bank accounts
while states borrow from the markets.

00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:50.408
Arrears build up out of sight and arrive
as a fiscal shock at year end.

00:00:50.458 --> 00:00:52.870
Utilisation certificates are late.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.767
Funders cannot link funding to outcomes.

00:00:55.817 --> 00:00:59.750
And auditors work from incomplete,
non-digital records.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.681
These gaps show up at every stage.

00:01:03.731 --> 00:01:05.754
Planning becomes incremental:

00:01:05.804 --> 00:01:08.399
small changes on last year's figures.

00:01:08.449 --> 00:01:11.473
Legislatures debate on provisional
numbers.

00:01:11.523 --> 00:01:17.335
In execution, uncertain fund flow leads to
float, delayed payments and litigation.

00:01:17.385 --> 00:01:22.769
Accounting and audit struggle with varying
practices and systems that do not

00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:23.341
connect.

00:01:23.391 --> 00:01:28.202
And nobody tracks the link from
expenditure to outputs and outcomes.

00:01:29.752 --> 00:01:31.644
The consequences are visible.

00:01:31.694 --> 00:01:34.523
Vendors and suppliers struggle to get
paid;

00:01:34.573 --> 00:01:38.943
in the most extreme cases, hospitals wait
for medicines or oxygen.

00:01:38.993 --> 00:01:42.491
Over time, good vendors stop working with
government,

00:01:42.541 --> 00:01:46.040
and those who remain charge a premium or
cut quality.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:50.995
And because systems do not talk to each
other, people re-key data by hand:

00:01:51.045 --> 00:01:55.214
slowly, with errors, and often the citizen
carries that burden.

00:01:56.764 --> 00:01:58.926
Underneath are two root causes.

00:01:58.976 --> 00:01:59.640
Data gaps:

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:04.635
records kept on paper, digitised late by
hand, or not recorded at all.

00:02:04.685 --> 00:02:06.776
And a lack of standardisation:

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:12.557
proprietary formats, the same term meaning
different things in different systems,

00:02:12.607 --> 00:02:15.554
no semantic or syntactic interoperability.

00:02:17.104 --> 00:02:20.293
At the heart of it all is one pivotal
problem.

00:02:20.343 --> 00:02:24.166
The flow of money is linked to the flow of
information.

00:02:24.216 --> 00:02:28.814
And the flow of information is slow,
incomplete, and inconsistent.

00:02:30.814 --> 00:02:31.186
Next:

00:02:31.236 --> 00:02:34.820
reimagining the flow of fiscal
information.
